e and that's because it's the major  antigen that's on the virion surface and  it's the only defined target of  neutralizing antibodies  so the spike proteins arranged as a  homogeneous it's a homo trimer of spike  monomers so i'm showing one of those to  you in green here  and it's the factor that's responsible  for driving cell entry  and it's got a few important domains  that'll be key to this talk one of them  is the receptor binding domain or rbd  that's responsible for engaging its  cellular receptor which is ace2  it is a viral fusion and so its fusion  machinery is housed within the s2 domain  and there's a third domain called the  ntd that i'd say has  poorly defined if any functions that are  clearly attributed to it at this point  in time  and this is unique  to other  to coronaviruses and is generally not  present in related viruses  so why are we interested in spike well  antibodies directed to spike are the  strongest correlated protection that we  have to date in evaluating vaccines or  protective immunity elicited by  infection  most of the vaccines that are being used  today and deployed or under clinical  evaluation are  based on the sequences of spike from the  emergency of virus which came about at  the end of 2019 or early in january 2020  and we began this work uh in the late  summer of 2020 as clinical trials were  in phase three and we were awaiting the  results and the question was was the  spike protein going to change or not  and there's two ways that we could  envision this one was it going to adapt  in some way to the new human host that  it was in  or as immunity because of the pandemic  was building in the populations would it  adapt to evade building immunity  and really the only well-characterized  mutation was this d614g mutation that  caught a lot of attention in that time  and it's not at any host interacting  interface and probably manipulates the  way the virus enters cells  and so if this spike then were to evolve  it would be 