u get longer and longer in  line  we then did a little bit more of a  sophisticated analysis  where we not only looked at maintaining  an open reading frame but those  deletions that resolved without the  introduction of a stop codon  and so  two out of every three deletions should  alter the reading frame and then a  certain percentage of those should  resolve with the stop codon and instead  what we saw overall for the deletion  variants that we identified that about  90 of them maintained an open reading  frame and i think this was the first  evidence that we had that this was real  and occurring in the context of  replication competent viruses  and also that the  selection was favoring these variants  that we were observing  and it's not uniformly true if we look  at the receptor binding domain or rbd  it's only about 30 percent of those  deletions resolve in frame without a  stop codon  uh similarly with s2 and it's really the  rdrs in the ntd that are heavily  enriched for deletions that maintain an  open reading frame  uh in the it drops down to about 50  outside of those domains within the ntd  so we wanted to know are these arising  individual patients or these being  transmitted between patients and so  using metadata that was deposited with  these sequences and the strict criteria  that the patients had to be  within the same country around the same  time  because we identif and they had to be  distinguishable so we needed to be able  to distinguish between two of the at  least two patients  we found clear examples of transmission  of each of a deletion at each one of  these sites so two sequences that were  identical in france  uh from a male and female of different  ages we found transmission clusters of  other rdr variants within senegal  ireland switzerland  and so what that last slide suggested is  that these were occurring all over the  world and in different genetic  backgrounds and that turns out to be  true so we looked at the geographic and  genetic distributions of these deletion  variants and again we saw two different  patterns  rdr1 and three behaved similarly where  they were strongly polarized for  specific genetic backgrounds and  specific geographies  whereas the diversity that we observe  for uh variants with deletions in rdrs 2  and 4  reflected generally speaking the genetic  diversity of the rest of the sequences  that had been deposited within the giza  database  so these deletions were recurrently  arising and the way that we showed this  is using phylogenetics so we took about  100 or so non-deletion viruses that  represented the extent of genetic  diversity of the pandemic within our  study window  and then we produced phylogenys by  aligning those sequences with the  different rdr variants that we had  and so  because these are phylogenetically  interleaved if you look at rdr2 there's  black lines that have no deletions and  red lines with deletions that are all  over the tree suggesting that they'd  arose independently multiple times over  this is true of the other ones  but if you look at rdrs  1 and 3 you can also see that variants  with deletions have been quite prolific  in their spread  suggesting that the deletion variance  can give rise to novel lineages  and we looked over time so throughout  all of this uh deletion variants have  been present at all phases of the  pandemic from the earliest times to to  the end of our study window and i can  say you know even circulating in nature  now  so you can do that by just having a  deletion present at those sites on the  left hand side or on the right-hand side  specific deletions that we saw over and  over again that were characteristic for  this virus  and i think would foreshadowed many of  the things that would come mind you this  was the fall of  2020 before variants had emerged you can  see that different variants of sarsko v2  with the deletion like a delta 6970  could really take off and giving  letting us know that the virus was  capable of making changes and running  with them  i'm a structural biologist by training  and so we looked structurally in these  rdrs mapped to antigenic sites within  the n-terminal domain  at the time we knew about antibody 4a8  and the entire epitope of this  antibodies formed by rdrs 2 and 4.  it doesn't make any contacts outside of  those  the rdr is at least mapped to some low  resolution em maps that christopher  barnes had produced for his work with  pamela bjorkman  so we we engineered different deletion  variants that we were observing both  within our patient and other long-term  infections or were enriched for within  the database into a spike expression  construct and we transfected viral cells  and we could stain those with a mouse  antibody directed to the receptor  binding domain all of them expressed  all of them have the presence of  multi-nucleated sensational bodies  and that tells us that all of these are  functional fusions  wild type cells are on the spike are on  the far right  uh we could then also state co-stain at  the same time with the human isotype of  antibody 488 and we can look at the  overlay below and i think what's quite  clear is that deletions within rdrs2 and  rdrs4  were independently sufficient to disrupt  binding of this antibody and so that  makes sense that these  loops that the antibodies engaging form  the entire epitope for it  using the virus that we isolated from  this long-term infection case  we were able to do neutralization  studies with antibody 4a  where the  viral swarm from that patient was  completely resistant to neutralization  whereas a wild type d614g virus this  munich p3 was entirely  or was neutralized to levels that were  previously reported  a flu specific antibody had no activity  for neutralization  also looked at a panel of convalescent  serums from patients that had survived  infections with sars cov2  it's broken up because the serums had  different potencies and neutralization  so we used similar dilutions with  similar potencies and neutralization on  the wild type virus  and  what we saw is that the mutations at  this site were insufficient to fully  resist neutralization by polyclonal  serum and so i think this was reassuring  to us that one or a few mutations was  insufficient to escape human immunity  so at the time what we knew of is  antibody 4a8 and another one 4-8 that  didn't have a full structure reported  for it  what we now know based on the work of a  lot of different groups is that that  corresponds to the ntd supersite so that  pattern of evolution that we saw was  within this super antigenic site that's  now been resolved in structural detail  but there are other sites within the ntd  that that have focused immune responses  like this site  or ones that overlap with it and when  you put all of the known ntd antibody  structures that had been deposited by  about august of this year  what you can see is that it's clearly  immunogenic there's a lot of different  antibodies that are contacting it in a  lot of different ways  and i think then it's not really  surprising that we're observing the fact  that the ntd is one of the more rapidly  evolving domains or is the most rapidly  evolving domain in the entire spike  protein  and so what i've talked about are these  deletions  what i didn't talk about are that there  are mutations that disrupt antibody  binding glycans have moved around so for  instance the p1 variant first identified  in brazil  has an additional glycan that blocks  some antibodies from binding  there are variants that circulate with  insertions within the ntd and even some  of those have created  new disulfide bonds between loops and  the signal peptides  so because a deletion  can alter multiple amino acids at the  same time  and alter the structures of  the  spike protein that they form it's not  really that surprising then that a  number of variants of concern that have  grabbed attention have incorporated  deletions to into their ntds  and what i'd like to point out is by  using our approach  of looking at patterns of evolution that  were recurrent before these variants  were identified we had already defined  clear phenotypes for what a deletion in  the rdr2 so a delta 144 or 145  or the deletion similar to the ones in  the beta variant that was first  identical identified in south africa so  that b.1.351  and so looking at the variation that  viruses can acquired and transmitted  between people allowed us to apply  figure out quite readily  genotypes and phenotypes for viruses as  sequencing information became available  one thing i'd like to touch back on is  the fact that thinking about this  long-term infection that we we  characterized there were two different  viruses that we we sequenced one with  the deletion at this 145 or 144 position  and then an adjacent position at 146.  and those represent two roads that the  virus took to achieving resistance to to  this four-eight antibody  not only that when we look at this viral  swarm that was isolated from this  patient the entirety of this warmer at  least the vast majority of it wasn't  neutralized because we saw no  neutralization and uh activity by  antibody 488  and in unpublished work what we've  looked at is a second long-term  infection case of somebody who had a  hodgkin lymphoma they were given an  anti-cd19 which would eliminate most of  the b cells  they were also infected for a long  period of time with sars kobe 2 and they  were treated with a monoclonal antibody  from from lily  so that right i can show you what the  structure  of that antibody is it's engaging the  receptor binding domain  and in sequencing that we've done what  we found are some viruses with this e44  q mutation and so this is well described  in the literature and was even found in  the clinical trial but we found a second  set of viruses also within the patient  that had this q um  439k mutation paired with this  serine  494 proline substitution  both of these have also been described  by others but what it does represent is  that the virus was taking multiple  different pathways to resist a  monoclonal antibody  and so while source cov2 may be  relatively slow to introduce mutations  it can readily overcome the focused  antibody response and i think that's an  important lesson is that just because  it's slow to  to mutate its genome it doesn't mean  it's evolutionarily stagnant  and so  this whole approach of using these minor  variants to understand fundamental  aspects about the virus and its  evolution i think is quite powerful and  so what we've been doing since  reporting this deletion story  is studying cases where there's focused  immunity and and looking at transmitted  variants and looking at the diversity  that underlies those sites to figure out  if this were to happen in the future in  a lineage of concern what what's the  consequence of that  and so we began this work with the  receptor binding domain or rbd and i'm  showing that to you in green it's bound  to its receptor h2 which is in white  and so one type of antibody response  that  focused is to this class iv epitope now  the reason why it's uh an interesting  target  is when you consider its conservation  across sarbaco viruses and so what i'm  showing to you on a surface  representation here's conservation  purple being the most conserved and so  the surfaces that these antibodies are  engaging are conserved across all  sarbaco viruses that have been reported  to date  so looking at diversity at this site and  looking for transmitted variants  uh we identified one transmission  cluster involving six or seven people in  the united kingdom they all produced  reasonable ct values that were detected  at the time  and why this is interesting is because  they harbored a deletion at that site so  three amino acids were missing within  that epitope  when we look at a sequence alignment  we can see the deletion right here  the amino acids 