 are occurring  and if we look more broadly uh across  sarbaco viruses this is a site that  tolerates an extreme amount of genetic  diversity  so the same patterns we're seeing in  stars kovi two evolution were observed  for uh multiple different bat and  pangolin or human sarbaco viruses  there are other sites in spike then that  do the same thing that are very tolerant  of insertions deletions mutations and  iterating cycles thereof and so the site  that i'm showing you here is the syrian  cleavage site  and there's been a lot of discussion as  to how the sars kobe 2 has a fear  insight and some other viruses related  to it don't  but i'd like to make the point and i  don't have answers for how any of this  came to be is that if you look carefully  enough what i'm showing you under rdr4  is that just out of thin air  a similar rrxr motif could just be  introduced by a random insertion right  there's four codons for uh glycine or or  alanine in the case of the natural one  there's six codons for arginine and so  these things will happen  now what i can say about the ones that  we that we're looking at in rdr4 it  doesn't appear to be processed as best  we can tell so reagents for that are a  little bit limited  um it didn't spread beyond a handful of  people and it's since gone extinct but  what it does show us is that iterating  cycles of insertions deletions and  mutations can produce  the types of mutations that could be  instantly beneficial for these sorts of  viruses and that's something to watch  for  and so just to wrap up i think deletions  do represent a rapid evolutionary  mechanism especially one that a virus  that has a proofreading polymerase can  employ  again you can't proofread a deletion  because there's nothing to compare  against  this clearly can alter the antigen  antigenicity of the spike glycoprotein  but we've also seen deletions come into  play where there's recurrent deletion of  specific residues within nsp6 which is  in the polymerase so that's been  acquired in a number of variants of  concern  and also anybody who's tried to grow  this virus on viral cells for a long  period of time that the virus doesn't  like its fear and cleavage site in those  cells and so it usually deletes it  rather than mutates it  what our work did shine light on to is  the fact that the ntd is very  immunogenic and rapidly evolving right  it's this malleable domain that can  tolerate an extreme amount of genetic  diversity  and this is going to be problematic for  antibodies that are directed to this  domain and how we think about vaccine  design and displaying the types of  proteins to the immune system that are  invariant versus those that are highly  variable  um and i do think that there is value in  looking at the tips of the phylogenetic  trees and understanding what's the  diversity that's being transmitted  person to person  uh even at low levels and what is that  telling us about the mutability of the  virus because if we've learned anything  it's that when the mutations that  benefit this virus are usually  recurrently then introduced right in the  case of n501y that's happened multiple  times over  similarly with e4k  similarly with the deletions again and  so because of the convergence of  evolution of these viruses  you know looking at the complex series  of changes that give genetic novelty i  think is quite informative  and so with that i'd like to thank a lot  of people who've contributed to this  work and their names will be up here on  the slide i'd like to thank all of you  for paying attention and i'll be  answering questions by email and  attending the session so thank you very  much

