hat were deposited at  that point in time and we identified  1100 of them or so that had deletions  within the spike gene  we then mapped their position and  lengths back onto spike and i think what  was quite striking is the fact that we  had four  hotspots for deletions  now we've named those recurrent deletion  regions or rdrs  one through four uh from five prime to  three prime  um and then i will say that you can see  a small bumps around where the junction  of uh s1 and s2 and that would be the  fearing cleavage site which also deletes  when these viruses are propagated  we can zoom in a little bit on the the  structure of what these look like and so  we noticed two trends here so one  typified by rdr1 and rvr3 where specific  deletions  or specific length deletions at specific  sites dominate the population  and that's why you get a strong peak in  the middle of each one  whereas for rdrs 2 and 4 we actually see  multiple deletions of variable lengths  and positions and so that builds up more  of a dome-like structure that made those  peaks on the previous slide  so we didn't  know what was going on here and we  hadn't heard anything else about anybody  reporting deletions and so we wanted to  convince ourselves that that this was  real  this was occurring in replication  competent viruses  and not some artifact  the first thing we quickly ruled out was  we didn't see any correlation of these  with sequencing platform and i'm not  showing those data to you today  but the simplest test was to say okay if  these were replicating viruses then they  should be able to make full length spike  proteins so so we created this histogram  that you're looking at here and integers  of three so three six nine should all be  more common because they would preserve  the reading frame  versus those that would inactivate it  causing a frame shift  and so that that turns out to be true  and if anything there's enrichment for  the deletions that preserve the reading  frame as yo