ilar dilutions with  similar potencies and neutralization on  the wild type virus  and  what we saw is that the mutations at  this site were insufficient to fully  resist neutralization by polyclonal  serum and so i think this was reassuring  to us that one or a few mutations was  insufficient to escape human immunity  so at the time what we knew of is  antibody 4a8 and another one 4-8 that  didn't have a full structure reported  for it  what we now know based on the work of a  lot of different groups is that that  corresponds to the ntd supersite so that  pattern of evolution that we saw was  within this super antigenic site that's  now been resolved in structural detail  but there are other sites within the ntd  that that have focused immune responses  like this site  or ones that overlap with it and when  you put all of the known ntd antibody  structures that had been deposited by  about august of this year  what you can see is that it's clearly  immunogenic there's a lot of different  antibodies that are contacting it in a  lot of different ways  and i think then it's not really  surprising that we're observing the fact  that the ntd is one of the more rapidly  evolving domains or is the most rapidly  evolving domain in the entire spike  protein  and so what i've talked about are these  deletions  what i didn't talk about are that there  are mutations that disrupt antibody  binding glycans have moved around so for  instance the p1 variant first identified  in brazil  has an additional glycan that blocks  some antibodies from binding  there are variants that circulate with  insertions within the ntd and even some  of those have created  new disulfide bonds between loops and  the signal peptides  so because a deletion  can alter multiple amino acids at the  same time  and alter the structures of  the  spike protein that they form it's not  really that surprising then that a  number of variants of concern that have  grabbed attention have incorporated  deletions to