tion  both of these have also been described  by others but what it does represent is  that the virus was taking multiple  different pathways to resist a  monoclonal antibody  and so while source cov2 may be  relatively slow to introduce mutations  it can readily overcome the focused  antibody response and i think that's an  important lesson is that just because  it's slow to  to mutate its genome it doesn't mean  it's evolutionarily stagnant  and so  this whole approach of using these minor  variants to understand fundamental  aspects about the virus and its  evolution i think is quite powerful and  so what we've been doing since  reporting this deletion story  is studying cases where there's focused  immunity and and looking at transmitted  variants and looking at the diversity  that underlies those sites to figure out  if this were to happen in the future in  a lineage of concern what what's the  consequence of that  and so we began this work with the  receptor binding domain or rbd and i'm  showing that to you in green it's bound  to its receptor h2 which is in white  and so one type of antibody response  that  focused is to this class iv epitope now  the reason why it's uh an interesting  target  is when you consider its conservation  across sarbaco viruses and so what i'm  showing to you on a surface  representation here's conservation  purple being the most conserved and so  the surfaces that these antibodies are  engaging are conserved across all  sarbaco viruses that have been reported  to date  so looking at diversity at this site and  looking for transmitted variants  uh we identified one transmission  cluster involving six or seven people in  the united kingdom they all produced  reasonable ct values that were detected  at the time  and why this is interesting is because  they harbored a deletion at that site so  three amino acids were missing within  that epitope  when we look at a sequence alignment  we can see the deletion right here  the amino acids 